{
  "requirement": "The `habeas` skill MUST tell agents that a change which both moves and edits code, tests, or requirements is done as the move first, under `habeas-refactor`, and then the edit, under the skill the edit routes to.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has one obligation. The `habeas` skill must tell agents that a change that both moves and edits code, tests, or requirements is done as the move first, under `habeas-refactor`, and then the edit, under the skill the edit routes to.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_habeas_skill_does_a_move_that_also_edits_as_the_move_first]` covers it. It takes numbered step 2 of the bundled `habeas` skill's Workflow section. It does this through `step` and `workflow_steps`, which read the skill's SKILL.md and collapse whitespace. It then uses `assert_text` to require the sentence: \"A change that both moves and edits code, tests, or requirements is the move first (habeas-refactor), then the edit under the skill the table gives for it\".\n\nThat phrase carries each element the requirement names:\n- the move-and-edit change, covering code, tests, and requirements;\n- the move comes first, under `habeas-refactor`;\n- the edit follows under the skill the routing table gives.\n\n`assert_text` panics if the phrase is missing, so the assertion is enforced. The phrase is in `assets/skills/habeas/SKILL.md`, in Workflow step 2, split across lines that `collapse` joins.\n\nThe requirement is about skill text, so a check that the text contains the phrase is the appropriate test. The scenario is a single static text, so no other input classes or modes apply. I found no gap.",
